 In contrast, a Local Area Network (LAN) is confined to a small geographical area like a house or office [Data: Entities (1465)]. A Metropolitan Area Network (MAN) bridges the gap, covering a metropolitan area such as a city or larger region [Data: Entities (1466)]. Lastly, a Personal Area Network (PAN) has an extremely limited reach, typically encompassing the area immediately surrounding an individual [Data: Entities (1468)]. Each of these classifications is directly linked to the concept of 'CLASSIFICACAO DE REDES POR ABRANGENCIA GEOGRAFICA' [Data: Relationships (2325, 2323, 2324, 2326)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Influence of Technology on Network Reach\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'Influence of Technology on Reach' (INFLUENCIA DA TECNOLOGIA NO ALCANCE) is a critical aspect of network design and implementation. It acknowledges that the communication technologies employed can significantly extend or limit a network's geographical reach, irrespective of its intended classification. This concept directly impacts the 'Geographical Reach' (ALCANCE GEOGRAFICO) of a network, illustrating a dynamic interplay between technology choices and spatial coverage [Data: Relationships (2328)]. For instance, while a LAN is inherently local, technologies can sometimes extend its perceived or actual range, and conversely, limitations in technology can restrict even a WAN's effectiveness.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Trade-offs in Network Design\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The design and implementation of computer networks involve inherent 'Trade-offs' (TRADE-OFFS EM REDES DE COMPUTADORES). These trade-offs often manifest between different characteristics such as speed, cost, and geographical reach. Improvements in one area may necessitate compromises in another. This principle is particularly evident when comparing network types, as detailed in the 'Comparative Analysis of Networks by Geographic Scope' (COMPARATIVO DE REDES POR ABRANGÊNCIA GEOGRÁFICA) [Data: Relationships (2407)]. For example, WANs offer vast reach but often at higher costs and potentially lower speeds compared to LANs, which are cost-effective and fast but limited in scope.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Specific Technologies Supporting Network Types\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Various technologies are integral to the operation and definition of different network types. LANs commonly utilize Ethernet and Wi-Fi for their high-speed, short-distance communication [Data: Entities (1465)]. MANs often employ fiber optic cables and high-speed Ethernet to achieve their city-wide connectivity [Data: Entities (1466)]. WANs, on the other hand, rely on technologies such as MPLS, satellite communication, and long-distance fiber optic cables, often leveraging telecommunications carriers [Data: Entities (1467)]. Furthermore, Cellular networks (REDE CELULAR), which use base stations distributed across geographical cells, can be utilized for WANs [Data: Entities (532), Relationships (2331)], and Satellite technology is also a key enabler for long-distance or remote WANs [Data: Entities (1469), Relationships (2332)]. PANs typically use Bluetooth, Wi-Fi Direct, and USB connections for close-proximity device communication [Data: Entities (1468)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Comparative Analysis of Network Characteristics\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The 'Comparative Analysis of Networks by Geographic Scope' (COMPARATIVO DE REDES POR ABRANGÊNCIA GEOGRÁFICA) provides a detailed examination of network types based on their geographical coverage. This analysis delves into the specific characteristics of each network, including LANs, which are highlighted as a distinct type within this comparative framework [Data: Relationships (2402)]. The comparison implicitly covers aspects like transmission speed, implementation cost, and common technologies associated with each network 